First Author: Singh
Title: Predicting Primary Graft Dysfunction in Systemic Sclerosis Lung Transplantation Using Machine-Learning and CT Features
Source number: 48  ￼

Very short summary: This single-center study developed machine-learning models to predict grade 3 primary graft dysfunction (PGD) at 72 hours after bilateral lung transplantation in systemic sclerosis recipients using preoperative clinical and CT-derived features. Five key findings were: (1) 92 systemic sclerosis recipients were included, and 20 (21.7%) developed PGD; (2) PGD was associated with BMI >30 kg/m², African American race, lower preoperative FEV1/FVC-related lung function, longer waitlist time, higher LAS, and ILD phenotype; (3) multiple CT-derived measures were significantly associated with PGD, including smaller recipient lung/chest cavity volume, higher heart-chest cavity ratio, cardiac adipose measures, and oversized donor-recipient size-matching ratios; (4) four ML algorithms were evaluated, with multilayer perceptron performing best; and (5) the best MLP model achieved AUROC 0.85 (95% CI 0.81–0.88) using four features: preoperative FEV1, heart-chest cavity volume ratio, waitlist time, and donor-to-recipient chest cavity volume ratio. Figures 1 and the ROC figure plus Tables 1–3 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

