Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a small single-center cohort of only 92 systemic sclerosis recipients, which limits generalizability and increases selection risk.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictors were clinically relevant preoperative variables plus detailed CT-derived cardiopulmonary and body-composition features extracted with previously validated deep-learning algorithms.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: PGD was explicitly defined using ISHLT criteria as pulmonary edema on chest X-ray plus PaO2/FiO2 < 200 at 72 hours.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include repeated stratified cross-validation, randomized hyperparameter search, multiple model classes, and multimodal feature integration. Uncertainty remains because of the small sample size and lack of external validation.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The article directly addresses machine-learning prediction of PGD after lung transplantation and is highly relevant, even though it focuses on the specific systemic sclerosis subgroup.  ￼

